{%- liquid
  assign s = section.settings
  assign product = s.product

  comment
    The live product price and the static price line are mutually exclusive, so
    the two can never disagree in front of a buyer.
  endcomment
  assign show_live_price = false
  if product != blank and s.show_price
    assign show_live_price = true
  endif
-%}

<section class="aurora aurora-hero" style="{% render 'aurora-spacing', settings: section.settings %}">
  <div class="aurora__inner aurora-hero__inner">
    <div class="aurora-hero__copy">
      {%- if s.eyebrow != blank -%}
        <p class="aurora__eyebrow aurora-reveal">{{ s.eyebrow }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}

      <h1 class="aurora__display aurora-hero__title aurora-reveal" style="--aur-reveal-delay: 80ms">
        {{ s.heading }}
      </h1>

      {%- if s.subheading != blank -%}
        <p class="aurora__lede aurora-reveal" style="--aur-reveal-delay: 160ms">{{ s.subheading }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%} One call to action. Nothing competes with it. {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="aurora-hero__action aurora-reveal" style="--aur-reveal-delay: 240ms">
        <a class="aurora__btn aurora__btn--primary" id="aurora-hero-cta" href="{{ s.cta_link | default: routes.all_products_collection_url }}">
          {{ s.cta_label }}
          {%- if show_live_price -%}
            <span class="aurora__price">{{ product.price | money }}</span>
          {%- endif -%}
        </a>

        {%- comment -%}
          The price sits at the point of decision. Without a product connected
          the button carries no figure at all, so the cost of the one thing this
          page is selling was never stated above the fold.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if show_live_price == false and s.price_note != blank -%}
          <div class="aur-price-signal">{{ s.price_note }}</div>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if s.assurance != blank -%}
          <p class="aurora-hero__assurance">{{ s.assurance }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      Deliberately not a .aurora-reveal. This image is the LCP element — it
      carries fetchpriority="high" — and fading it in holds the largest paint
      back by the length of the transition, for motion that neither guides
      attention nor communicates state nor preserves continuity.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="aurora-hero__media">
      {%- if s.image != blank -%}
        {{
          s.image
          | image_url: width: 1400
          | image_tag:
            widths: '500, 800, 1100, 1400',
            sizes: '(max-width: 989px) 100vw, 48vw',
            loading: 'eager',
            fetchpriority: 'high',
            alt: s.image.alt | default: s.heading | strip_html
        }}
      {%- else -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Ships with real product photography rather than a grey placeholder, so
          the theme is presentable the moment it is installed. Replace it by
          setting a Product image in the theme editor.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <img
          src="{{ 'aurora-hero-rings.jpg' | asset_url }}"
          width="1200"
          height="1262"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          alt="Three Aurora rings in black, rose gold and copper finishes"
        >
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{% stylesheet %}
  .aurora-hero {
    padding-block: var(--padding-block-start, 0) var(--padding-block-end, 0);
  }

  .aurora-hero__inner {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: clamp(1.75rem, 5vw, 5rem);
    align-items: center;
    padding-block: clamp(1.5rem, 8vh, 6rem);
  }

  .aurora-hero__media img,
  .aurora-hero__media svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* No blend and no tint on the photograph, deliberately.

     The asset's backdrop samples at rgb(245, 244, 240) against a page of
     rgb(246, 244, 240) — it was shot and graded to this exact page tone, so it
     already meets the page with no edge, and there is nothing here for a
     mix-blend-mode to fix.

     It is also why the atmosphere below is kept off the product. Any tone
     behind the frame that is deeper than the backdrop brings the rectangle
     back, and re-keying it with darken would cost the photograph its
     highlights: measured against the deeper bone, that clips 11.5% of the
     product's pixels and takes up to 25/255 off the rose gold's speculars.
     A background effect does not get to dull the product on a jewellery
     page. */

  /* Phones: type first, then the photograph in its own full-width band.
     The previous treatment laid the copy over a full-height cover crop of the
     asset, which produced two faults that pulled against each other. Cover
     against a 0.53-ratio viewport with a 0.95-ratio asset scales to fill the
     height and throws away ~45% of the frame's width, so only the middle ring
     survived the crop; and the copy then had to be pushed into the lower half
     to clear what was left of the product, which is why the eyebrow started
     mid-screen. Neither could be fixed without making the other worse.
     Stacking settles both at once: the type starts at the top of the section,
     and the photograph renders at its own aspect ratio underneath, so all
     three rings read whole. The action still lands above the fold. */
  @media screen and (max-width: 989px) {
    /* Atmosphere, kept behind the type and clear of the frame. A soft lift in
       the deeper bone sits under the headline and has fully resolved back to
       the page tone by 55% — above where the photograph starts — so the
       product is always on the one tone its backdrop was graded to.

       Radial rather than a top-to-bottom ramp for the same reason: every edge
       of the section returns to the page tone, so it meets the header above
       and the trust bar below with no seam, which a linear ramp cannot do
       without leaving a step at one end. */
    .aurora.aurora-hero {
      background:
        radial-gradient(130% 34% at 50% 6%, var(--aur-page-alt), transparent 55%),
        var(--aur-bg);
    }

    .aurora-hero__inner {
      gap: 0;
      padding-block: clamp(1.25rem, 4vh, 2rem) 0;
    }

    /* Edge to edge, so the photograph is the width of the phone rather than a
       card inset by the gutter. Expressed as one margin pair rather than as a
       relative offset: the box's own layout width stays 100vw and its used
       position lands on the viewport edges, so nothing overhangs the document
       and no horizontal scroll appears.

       Tucked up under the copy as well, to close the dead space that made the
       two halves read as two sections rather than one composition.

       The lift is sized from the asset, not by eye. The frame carries 70px of
       empty backdrop above its topmost ring — 5.5% of its height — and at this
       ratio the frame is 1.052x the viewport width, so that band is 5.78vw. A
       5vw lift takes nearly all of it and still leaves clearance; 6vw would
       have started cutting into the rose gold. There is no matching band at
       the bottom: the plinths run to the frame's edge, which is also why a
       synthetic contact shadow would have had nowhere to go. */
    .aurora-hero__media {
      width: 100vw;
      margin-inline: calc(50% - 50vw);
      margin-top: -5vw;
    }

    /* Positioned only so the copy stays above the photograph in paint order,
       since the frame is pulled up under it. */
    .aurora-hero__copy {
      position: relative;
    }

    /* Poster scale. The display token floors at 2.75rem, and 8vw does not
       clear that floor until about 550px wide — so on every phone the headline
       was being set by the floor rather than by the viewport, and arrived the
       same size it takes on a tablet. Sized from the viewport here, against a
       shorter lede below, the step between the two is a step rather than a
       slope.

       Scoped through the inner so it outranks .aurora__display, which sets the
       same three properties from the globally rendered token snippet — equal
       specificity there would leave the winner down to stylesheet order. */
    .aurora-hero__inner .aurora-hero__title {
      font-size: clamp(3.25rem, 14vw, 5rem);
      line-height: 0.9;
      letter-spacing: -0.03em;
      max-width: 14ch;
    }

    /* One line rather than two — at the token's tracking the eyebrow wrapped on
       every phone, which put a broken label above the headline — and a hairline
       under it. The rule is the same structural device the ownership columns
       and the box list already use, and it gives the copy a masthead to open
       from instead of three type sizes stacked in a column. */
    .aurora-hero__inner .aurora__eyebrow {
      font-size: 0.6875rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.1em;
      margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
      padding-bottom: 1rem;
      border-bottom: 1px solid var(--aur-line);
    }

    .aurora-hero__inner .aurora__lede {
      font-size: 1rem;
      max-width: 32ch;
    }

    /* Scoped through the inner for the same reason as the title. The base
       .aurora-hero__action rule is declared after this block at equal
       specificity, so an unscoped override here would lose to it on source
       order and the phone spacing would silently never apply — which is what
       was happening before. */
    .aurora-hero__inner .aurora-hero__action {
      margin-top: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 2rem);
    }
  }

  /* Landscape phones only. Portrait has the height to run the photograph at
     its own ratio, and capping it there is what inset the frame and shrank the
     product. Landscape genuinely does not have the height, so the cap stays
     for that case — contain rather than cover, so the three rings survive
     whole either way, and the bars it leaves are the page tone the backdrop
     already matches, which makes them invisible. */
  @media screen and (max-width: 989px) and (orientation: landscape) {
    .aurora-hero__media img,
    .aurora-hero__media svg {
      max-height: 70vh;
      max-height: 70svh;
      object-fit: contain;
    }
  }

  .aurora-hero__title {
    max-width: 12ch;
  }

  .aurora-hero__action {
    margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
  }

  .aurora-hero__assurance {
    margin: 1.125rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--aur-text-muted);
  }

  .aurora-hero__placeholder {
    background: var(--aur-page-alt);
    fill: rgba(20, 21, 26, 0.12);
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 990px) {
    .aurora-hero__inner {
      /* The product gets the larger share — it is the reason anyone is here */
      grid-template-columns: 0.9fr 1.1fr;
      gap: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 5rem);
      /* No min-height. An 82vh floor made the grid row taller than either
         column, and the inherited align-items: center then split the surplus
         evenly above and below the copy — 218px of dead space over the eyebrow
         at 1440x900. Sizing the row from its content removes the surplus at
         source, so the offset below is the only thing setting the top edge. */
      align-items: start;
      padding-block: var(--aur-hero-top) var(--aur-hero-bottom);
    }

    /* A deliberate drop, so the headline reads against the upper third of the
       product rather than butting against the grid line. */
    .aurora-hero__copy {
      padding-block-start: var(--aur-hero-top);
    }

    /* Contained, not bled. Running the photograph past the container clipped
       the lower-right ring mid-band, which read as an accident rather than as
       a crop. All three rings now sit inside the column, whole. */
    .aurora-hero__media {
      margin-right: 0;
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Aurora hero",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "aurora-section",
  "disabled_on": { "groups": ["header", "footer"] },
  "settings": [
    { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow", "default": "The smart ring without the subscription" },
    {
      "type": "inline_richtext",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading",
      "default": "Sleep is the <em>whole</em> game."
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "subheading",
      "label": "Subheading",
      "default": "Aurora measures your nights and tells you one thing each morning: push, or hold back. Buy it once and every insight is yours for good."
    },
    { "type": "image_picker", "id": "image", "label": "Product image" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Action" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "cta_label", "label": "Button label", "default": "Buy Aurora" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "cta_link", "label": "Button link" },
    { "type": "product", "id": "product", "label": "Product (for live price)" },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "show_price", "label": "Show price in button", "default": true },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "price_note",
      "label": "Price line",
      "info": "Shown under the button when no product is connected above.",
      "default": "€249 (VAT incl.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "assurance",
      "label": "Assurance line",
      "default": "Free EU delivery · Free size exchange · No subscription, ever"
    },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Padding" },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-start",
      "label": "Top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 200,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-end",
      "label": "Bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 200,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 24
    }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Aurora hero", "category": "Aurora" }]
}
{% endschema %}
